"""Replace the abstract-based account of four papers with the full-text reading. Run after patch_search_ress.py."""
from pathlib import Path
p=Path("submission-ress/manuscript.tex");T=p.read_text()
R=[("Kulkarni and Digalwar \\cite{kulkarni2026} use two rolling folds with each test failure unseen and report that both failures are detected, with detection depending on the training cut-off. Their positive rows lie inside the failure windows, so this is detection rather than warning. Other papers report lead time: the abstracts of Chakri et al.\\ \\cite{chakri2026} give 17 to 42 minutes before the four events, and those of Alhammadi et al.\\ \\cite{alhammadi2025} and Silva et al.\\ \\cite{silva2023} give up to two hours. We read only the abstracts of these three, so we cannot say how their windows or thresholds were set.",
"Kulkarni and Digalwar \\cite{kulkarni2026} use two rolling folds with each test failure unseen. Their positive rows lie inside the failure windows, and both failures are detected at the first labelled row, so this is detection rather than warning. They also show that detection depends on the training cut-off: a model trained to 15 May scores 99\\% of the June failure rows above the threshold and none of the July rows, and one trained to 13 July raises no alarm on the July rows at the selected threshold. That is non-transfer on the same data by a different route.\n\nThree papers report lead time, under definitions that differ from ours. Chakri et al.\\ \\cite{chakri2026} credit a flag inside a failure or within 30 minutes before it, and report flagged runs that begin 17 to 42 minutes before the four events. Alhammadi et al.\\ \\cite{alhammadi2025} test failure rows against an equal span of the normal data just before them. Their two hours comes from the 15 July failure alone, and those early flags count as false positives in their own metrics. Silva et al.\\ \\cite{silva2023} use the 2022 release, with two failures, and call a warning early if it comes two hours before the low-pressure signal rather than before the failure report. Only Kulkarni and Digalwar report alarm episodes per day, and we saw no chance control for lead time in the sections we read, so these figures are not comparable with our catch rule."),
("although a sustained loaded run so close to each start argues against a long lag.",
"although a sustained loaded run so close to each start argues against a long lag on three of the four failures.\n\nThe exception is 15 July. Kulkarni and Digalwar \\cite{kulkarni2026} report a first alarm 5.4 hours before that failure, and Alhammadi et al.\\ \\cite{alhammadi2025} report early flags of 1 to 2 hours. Our telemetry shows a possible reason: the compressor was loaded for 48\\% of the six hours before that report, against a median of 10\\% in report-free windows, and only 3.9\\% of those windows reach that level. The share was 1\\%, 13\\% and 14\\% before the other three. This may be a real precursor, or a report that lagged the physical onset, or ordinary heavy use. One event cannot separate these, the check is exploratory, and our models, trained on the other failures, raised no warning of it."),
("and we read some close conference papers in abstract only.","and some records were screened in abstract only."),
]
for a,b in R:
    assert T.count(a)==1,a
    T=T.replace(a,b)
p.write_text(T);print(len(R),"replacements")
